"bm_george|Byte\u2019s memo: The flood lot":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|The lot sits at the low end of the street, so rain from about 140 homes runs downhill to it.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|In a big monsoon storm, more than 1.5 inches of rain can fall in about an hour.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|The street drain can only handle about 1.4 inches, so in every big storm the extra water floods 23 homes.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|A detention basin is a low place that holds storm water for a few hours and then lets it drain away slowly.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|A skate bowl can be a detention basin too: dry for skating most days, and full only after big storms.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|The deeper the basin and the wider its drain, the bigger the storm it can hold.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|Phoenix is one of the hottest big cities in America, with more than 100 days a year above 100\u00b0F.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|In July sun, bare concrete can get hotter than 140\u00b0F, and shade can make it 30 or more degrees cooler.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|Without shade, almost nobody uses a park here between late morning and sunset.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|Robot crews build at night, when it is cooler and fresh concrete cures more evenly.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|The first big storm of the summer is forecast for Thursday night, and I am about 70 in 100 sure.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|My city maps show storm water reaching the lot only from the street side.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|Updated: Mrs. Okafor says the old irrigation ditch in the alley spills onto the lot in about 3 of 10 big storms, so every design now takes water from both sides.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|City flood log: the lot":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|Arroyo, the city\u2019s stormwater AI, runs about 4,000 drain gates, pumps and basins across Phoenix.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|In a storm it acts on its own, in seconds, following rules that the city engineer signs each year.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|Last summer, the street by the lot flooded on 4 days.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|Two of those floods came after big storms, of 1.7 and 2.1 inches.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|The other two came after small storms, under 1 inch, and both started at the back of the lot.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|Both small-storm floods began on a Tuesday night.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|Arroyo marked their cause as unknown.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|Neighbor survey: what should the lot be?":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|Last night I asked the 200 households nearest the lot what they would like there.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|52 out of 100 picked a shady garden, and 41 picked a skate park.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|Dozens wrote the same three words: why not both?":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|Older neighbors asked for shade, benches with backs, and a smooth ramp instead of steps.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|Teens asked for a real bowl, lights for skating after dark, and a water fountain.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|The Ledger: Phoenix":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|Phoenix now has more robots than people, and most of them work in the robot zone west of the city.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|Robot crews put up 40 new shade sails along the Grand Canal trail this month.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|A new trail sign explains that some of today\u2019s canals follow routes the Hohokam people dug more than 1,000 years ago.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|Satellite photos show a shiny silver patch at Saltbrush Flats, just past the edge of the robot zone, that has doubled in size in one week.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|The county says no one has a permit to mine there.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|monsoon. A season when the winds shift and bring storms. In Phoenix, monsoon storms come mostly in July, August and September.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|detention basin. A low place built to hold storm water for a few hours, then let it drain away slowly, so streets don\u2019t flood.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|bowl. A skate bowl: a smooth, rounded concrete hollow, like an empty swimming pool, that skaters ride around.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|stormwater. Rain that runs off roofs, streets and yards during a storm.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|irrigation. Bringing water to plants through ditches or pipes. Some Phoenix neighborhoods still flood their yards with canal water on set nights.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|cures. When concrete cures, it slowly hardens. If it dries too fast in the heat, it can crack.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|ramp. A gentle slope instead of steps, so wheelchairs, walkers and strollers can roll up easily.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|hohokam. People who lived in the Phoenix area long ago and dug hundreds of miles of canals to water their farms.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|haboob. A huge wall of dust pushed across the desert by cold wind rushing out of a thunderstorm.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|permit. Official permission to do something, like build or dig, after the plan has been checked.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|robot zone. An area set aside for factories and robot work. In 2040, heavy industry must stay inside these zones, and new robots need permits.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|satellite. A machine that circles the Earth. Some take photos of the ground from space.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|red team. People (or AIs) who try to break or misuse a plan on purpose, so its weak spots get fixed before anyone else finds them.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|least privilege. Giving a person, program or robot only the permissions its job really needs.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
